<p>Talking about <strong>Japanese Wagyu regions</strong> can be confusing because prefectures, cities, breeds, bloodlines and brands are often mixed together. Kobe is not a breed; Miyazaki is a prefecture as well as the name behind a beef programme; Tajima is a Japanese Black population associated with Hyogo; and Matsusaka is a brand with its own production requirements.</p>
<p>Japan has hundreds of regional Wagyu brands. You do not need to memorise them all, but understanding the major names makes product descriptions much easier to interpret.</p>
<h2>Region is not the same as grade</h2>
<p>A regional brand and A5 answer different questions. A5 describes carcass grading. A regional programme defines origin and its own production conditions. Products from different regions can both be A5.</p>
<h2>Hyogo: Tajima and Kobe Beef</h2>
<p><strong>Hyogo</strong> is central to Wagyu history. Tajima cattle, a Japanese Black population, underpin several famous brands. Kobe Beef is the best-known international name associated with Hyogo, but not every piece of beef from the prefecture qualifies as Kobe.</p>
<h2>Mie: Matsusaka Beef</h2>
<p><strong>Mie</strong> is home to Matsusaka Beef, one of Japan's historic prestige brands. Its programme is associated with Japanese Black females raised under defined conditions in the designated area.</p>
<h2>Shiga: Omi Beef</h2>
<p><strong>Omi Beef</strong> from Shiga has a long history in Japanese beef culture and is often mentioned alongside Kobe and Matsusaka. Its value lies not in a simple ranking but in a defined regional identity.</p>
<h2>Miyazaki: a major Japanese Black producer</h2>
<p><strong>Miyazaki</strong> in Kyushu is an important Japanese Black producing prefecture. Miyazakigyu has strong recognition at home and abroad. “Miyazaki” identifies origin/programme; “A5” identifies carcass grade.</p>
<h2>Kagoshima: scale and prestige in Kyushu</h2>
<p><strong>Kagoshima</strong> is another major cattle-producing area and Kagoshima Wagyu appears frequently in premium export markets. Comparing it with Miyazaki or Kobe only makes sense when grade, BMS and cut are also considered.</p>
<h2>Gifu: Hida Beef</h2>
<p><strong>Hida Beef</strong> from Gifu is a well-known Japanese Black regional brand. It demonstrates that a beef does not need the Kobe name to represent established regional production and high standards.</p>
<h2>Yamagata: Yonezawa Beef</h2>
<p><strong>Yonezawa Beef</strong> comes from Yamagata in northern Honshu and has a strong reputation for carefully produced, richly marbled beef.</p>
<h2>Iwate and northern Japan</h2>
<p>Iwate offers both Japanese Black brands such as Maesawa and production of Japanese Shorthorn, a much leaner Wagyu breed with a more meat-forward profile.</p>
<h2>Kumamoto and Kochi: Japanese Brown</h2>
<p>Japanese Brown, or Akaushi, is particularly associated with <strong>Kumamoto and Kochi</strong>. It generally offers a different lean-to-fat balance from Japanese Black.</p>
<h2>Yamaguchi: Japanese Polled</h2>
<p>Japanese Polled is a very rare Wagyu breed associated particularly with <strong>Yamaguchi</strong>. Its existence is a reminder that Wagyu extends far beyond highly marbled Japanese Black.</p>
<h2>Restaurant location is not beef origin</h2>
<p>A Tokyo or Osaka restaurant can serve Kobe, Miyazaki, Hida or many other brands. The location of the retailer or restaurant should never be confused with the cattle's origin.</p>
<h2>How regional brands work</h2>
<p>Each organisation defines its own conditions, which may cover geographic area, breed, rearing period, authorised producers, sex and minimum quality grade. Definitions therefore need to be checked brand by brand.</p>
<h2>Which is better: Kobe, Miyazaki or Kagoshima?</h2>
<p>There is no universal answer. Cut, grade, BMS, format and personal taste can matter more than brand fame. See our <a href="/en/kobe-miyazaki-kagoshima-japanese-wagyu-differences/">Kobe, Miyazaki and Kagoshima comparison</a>.</p>
<h2>Using origin to buy better</h2>
<p>Confirm Japanese origin first. Then identify the prefecture or regional brand and understand its rules. Finally compare grade, BMS, cut, weight, storage and price.</p>
<h2>The conclusion</h2>
<p><strong>Japan does not produce one single Wagyu style but a huge range of regional brands built on different breeds, bloodlines, environments and standards.</strong> Hyogo/Kobe, Mie/Matsusaka, Shiga/Omi, Miyazaki, Kagoshima, Gifu/Hida and Yamagata/Yonezawa are major reference points, but only part of the map.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is Kobe a region or breed?</h3><p>Kobe Beef is a regional brand connected to Hyogo and Tajima cattle, not a separate breed.</p>
<h3>Are Miyazaki and Kagoshima Wagyu?</h3><p>They are prefectures with major Wagyu programmes, primarily Japanese Black.</p>
<h3>Which three names are often cited as historic prestige brands?</h3><p>Kobe, Matsusaka and Omi are frequently grouped together.</p>
<h3>Does region alone determine quality?</h3><p>No. Grade, BMS, cut, producer and traceability also matter.</p>
